\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The retained variance detects the forgotten weight}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{For the [two-vertex family](fdip-004O), eliminate the coordinate
at vertex #{1}. The [positive Schur formula](fdip-000D) and
[retained covariance](fdip-000E) give
##{\begin{aligned}
S_w&=(1+w)-\frac{w^2}{1+w}
     =\frac{1+2w}{1+w}>0,\\
r(\mathcal B_w)&=S_w^{-1}=\frac{1+w}{1+2w}.
\end{aligned}}
In particular,
##{r(\mathcal B_1)=\frac23\ne\frac35=r(\mathcal B_2),
\qquad c(\mathcal B_1)=c(\mathcal B_2)=U.}
The [fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) excludes a function
#{F:c(X)\to\mathbb R} satisfying #{r=F\circ c}, even on a
restricted family containing these two backgrounds. Consequently
the unweighted interval with its retained endpoint also cannot
recover this variance on that family. This is an obstruction for
the specified forgetting and observable, not for every geometric
realization.}

\p{This two-coordinate calculation is a teaching variant of the
[weighted-cycle comparison](fdip-000I), which already gives unequal
retained correlations #{2/15} and #{16/105} at fixed mass and
fixed retained pair. The same principle is visible with one retained
coordinate: incidence and the selected endpoint do not retain
the weighted precision. By contrast,
[weight-preserving relabelings](fdip-000G), with the retained
projection and observable transported together, preserve expectations
by [Gaussian transport](fdip-000H). Enlarging their comparison to
forget weights need not preserve them.}
